"Marks every observation with a short tick on a common numeric axis, usually as a compact marginal display.\n\nUse when: A modest sample should remain visible beneath another distribution summary. Readers need to see gaps, ties, or exact observed locations.\n\nAvoid when: Thousands of overlapping ticks would turn the display into an unreadable solid band.\n\nVisual grammar: Tick position encodes the observed quantitative value. Tick multiplicity or overlapping darkness indicates repeated values only approximately.\n\nBuild: Place one equal-length tick at each valid measured value. Reserve a narrow strip beneath the main axis or a companion plot. Add sample size and explain any transparency or repeated-value treatment.\n\nCheck: Check every observation produces a tick or a documented aggregation. Verify tick lengths do not imply a second numeric measure. Keep tick positions on the same scale as the companion chart.\n\nPitfalls: Overplotting hides duplicates and understates local counts. Do not jitter ticks along the measured axis, because that changes values.\n\nExample: Add a rug beneath a synthetic transaction-size density curve to expose gaps and isolated purchases.\n\nAlternatives: Strip plot, Density plot, Histogram."
